<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Khazneh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as The Treasury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Khazneh, 'the Treasury', is a monumental facade carved into the sandstone cliff at the end of the Siq gorge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, Jordan. Built probably in the 1st century CE, perhaps as the tomb of the Nabataean king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a>, it combines Hellenistic columns and sculpture with Nabataean features. Local legend held that a treasure was hidden in the urn at its top.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khazneh.jpg" alt="Al-Khazneh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Susanahajer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_khazneh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
